#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163232 :tutorial/content "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix $$A =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$$ has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}_{A} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{U} \\: \\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\\Sigma} \\: \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]^T}_{V^T}$$\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae8b1a4c-c37e-5527-9f72-9cf20f5af926" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4892 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163246 :tutorial/content "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula $$\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,$$ where $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n$$A =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}], \\quad \\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$$\\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b48ac70-7c4e-59be-bdfb-64125ae50eec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5010 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding a Singular Value Decomposition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163247 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and\n\n$$A= U\\Sigma V^T$$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition.\n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$A^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$ $${\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}$$ So, the corresponding singular values are $${\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} = 4}, \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain $$A^T \\! A - 16I = [\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}] - 16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].$$ Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get $${\\color{blue}{ [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}}.$$\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain $$A^T \\! A - I = [\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}] - [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].$$ Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get $${\\color{red} [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}.$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaa68b2-45d9-56a5-99b7-90dce5ec08da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5011 :tutorial/title "Finding the Second and Third Matrices of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163248 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix, and\n\n![](images/ec/ec033e8518a97ff363494df7effa3c6f032b7cfaa028712b87959d659ea12962.svg) is a singular value decomposition of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Let's now describe how to compute the matrix ![](images/c3/c3ae10e66e0dee45c359329a1cd8b0246634f95fdce2f5bf206d04dac3594fc5.svg) of the SVD using the matrices ![](images/12/128e4b474ccbb86ef9b1a105a348e493595f4084d05e9cfa5f7889d390d5eac4.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d936a1af541b15fe85bff8b60fbe1d8d1851f9e91616cf4799dd0c5323d038a.svg)\n\nRecall that ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) is orthogonal, meaning that ![](images/c7/c71eaab408a38ca1a1d0c653fe51ab02287d8ffbe4ef0461c93186579552d456.svg) Consequently, multiplying ![](images/c9/c99b0facd0ff831056deed9aaa5356ca5cbeed72ed9f632604cd5fb9479c3b7a.svg) by ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) on the right, we obtain\n\n![](images/03/036ae766d4187370271793b6bbacc9841b444173ff031740acbd259531ed5126.svg)\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b9/b924929217b058d3edefff9a6557d3ad8b5d6b3536e7ac0dcaf185e50b60bb5a.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/64/64b1897c8532aee8fee0d4f76f632cc91c6a5a4078b11bfaaea07235aeadcc3f.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/fe/fedbe16eb8c09802bf435c256bb225249c1ee578dd558d0f3b6b284a56236517.svg) Finally, provided that ![](images/66/668ce05b1a81d3d120c47a7dadea446891ef986edb5f499c592f310755bb143c.svg) we obtain\n\n![](images/ec/ecd9fe71eaef2172512d70bfb78ffea6e36b973e6f96c09f0480ef2e0e9e55ad.svg)\n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9d412fae57d02f4fc44b04a277e1a6512e3f560e3152416a69d30d72e38a9ac.svg) Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b577630f-1060-52c1-aabe-91b641046fa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5012 :tutorial/title "Finding the First Matrix of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163317 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below.\n\n$$A =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}], \\quad \\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\quad V = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$\n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04644a93-8cce-5c2e-b575-d7f05b4985df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6135 :tutorial/title "Calculating the First Matrix - A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163364 :tutorial/content "Let $A = U \\Sigma V^T$ be a singular value decomposition of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with singular values $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2.$\n\nDue to the decomposition, the action of $A$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the same as the action of the sequence $V^T \\to \\Sigma \\to U.$\n\nAs shown below, let's consider both equivalent actions on a unit circle (with the two standard basis vectors).\n\n![](images/18/18bf28d59138e6c50e950493570a4e9caa8ec2f86eb81d573272cb4677ea1d69.png)\n\n- Since $V^T$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a transformation of the plane that preserves distances between vectors. This could be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of these two, which is sometimes called a **rotoreflection.**\n- Since $\\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix, it represents a scaling along the $x$- and $y$-axes by $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2,$ respectively.\n- Since $U$ is again an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a rotation, a reflection, or a rotoreflection.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Any linear transformation on the plane can be decomposed into an orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection), a scaling, and another orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection).*" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cadc01a-8396-532a-a438-479affeb9a04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7033 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Singular Value Decomposition"}]}
